Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-31-2001||06:35 AM||||Most of the time headliner boards are pretty easy to fix.  Buy a fiberglass repair kit from your local auto parts store.  This should come with a bunch of fiberglass matte, one of those plastic applicators (throw this out), a large can of resin and a small tube of hardener.  Lay your headliner board out on a flat surface and use a broom to brush off the remains of the old material and glue.  Mix some of the resin and hardener (mix according to the directions) and, using a regular paint brush (not one of those foam ones), start painting the resin onto the board.  Don't overdo it, but make sure it's fully covered enough to soak into the board.  For the places that have either cracked or broken completely, cut a piece of fiberglass matte to completely cover the broken area (leave a few inches on either side), soak this in your bowl of resin mix and lay on the crack/break.  Paint more resin over this.  Let all of this harden overnight.  Repeat on the other side.  If you do it right, your board will be better and stronger than when it was brand new.  Also, the resin will give the new headliner material a smoother surface to adhere to.  I wouldn't waste my time trying to find another one at a boneyard unless yours is in many, many pieces.  Most likely, you'll have to fix/repair any that you get.<P>On the other hand, if you have plenty of money, you can buy a fiberglass board from The Fiero Store for a little less than $200US, but I think you'll have to make the 3rd brake light cut out (if needed).<P>If you have any specific questions, feel free to PM/email me and I'll try to help.  I just did this to the headliner board in 4Play.  It's not as difficult as it sounds.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jonathan||08-31-2001||06:51 AM||||I replaced my headliner without the fiber board.  Mine broke into four pieces when a gust of wind threw it against a tree trunk.  I bought padded headliner fabric, about as thick as a mouse pad, from a local fabric store.  $3.99/yd for black.  One yard was sufficient.  Then bought headliner adhesive from the autoparts store.  $6.99.  Installed it as one piece, and then used an exacto knife to trim for the sunroof, windows, etc.  Most people in the car don't notice that its not original, and it looks a lot better than the bare roof that I drove around with for so long.  (Be careful around the sunroof.  If you trim too close, you won't have enough to tuck under the trim gasket.)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fierotag2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
